\title{\textbf{Reward, Reset, and Evaluation Design for\\Sim-to-Real Reinforcement Learning:\\A Case Study in Quadrotor Gate Racing}}
\author{Zhuohao Ni \and Bruce (Yuqian) Zhang}
\date{}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
\noindent
We report on a two-phase reinforcement learning project in which a single
quadrotor racing task---a seven-waypoint course containing a
self-intersecting double-gate structure---was first solved as a pure
simulation problem and then re-solved as a transfer problem targeting a
physical vehicle tracked by motion capture. The two phases share a track, a
simulator, and a learning algorithm, but almost nothing else: the reward
function, the observation space, the reset distribution, the randomization
strategy, and the checkpoint-selection rule were each re-derived, and in four
of five cases inverted, when the simulator stopped being the objective and
became a hypothesis about a system we could only sample expensively.

In the simulation phase, dense shaping with hand-placed virtual targets
reached $97.3\%$ three-lap success at a mean time of $15.68$\,s across
$5000$ randomized environments. In the sim-to-real phase, a sparse
gate-centric reward with an observation space constrained by the deployment
interface reached $98.0\%$ success at $18.34$\,s under a re-identified
dynamics nominal, $100\%$ at $18.10$\,s under a deployment-matched start
distribution, and completed the physical three-lap evaluation in
$19.004$\,s, continuing to five correctly ordered laps.

We argue that three design objects usually treated as implementation details
carry most of the transfer risk: the reward predicate's relationship to the
task's progress predicate, the reset distribution as an explicit choice of
where the value function is fit, and the evaluation harness, which failed for
us in both directions---once by being more diverse than deployment, and once
by being calibrated so closely to measured hardware that it ranked a policy
first that managed a single lap in the arena.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Reinforcement learning for agile flight is usually presented as a transfer
problem: train in simulation, randomize the physics, deploy
\cite{loquercio2020,kaufmann2023,song2023}. Presented that way, the
interesting variable is the randomization distribution, and the reported
result is a success rate. This framing hides the fact that a policy trained
against a simulator whose fidelity is not in question and a policy trained
against a simulator standing in for hardware are solutions to different
optimization problems, even when the Markov decision process is written
identically.

This report documents a project in which we solved both. The task is fixed
throughout: fly three laps of a seven-waypoint gate course, in order, without
crashing. In the \emph{simulation phase}, correctness was defined by the
simulator, and the rational strategy was to shape the reward as aggressively
as training stability allowed---dense progress terms, velocity blending that
induced racing lines implicitly, and hand-placed virtual targets that steered
the policy through the hardest section of the course. In the
\emph{sim-to-real phase}, the same simulator became a model whose parameters
we did not know, whose observation pipeline had a physical counterpart with
its own biases, and whose evaluation numbers had to predict an outcome we
could measure only a handful of times.

Four design decisions inverted between the phases:

\begin{enumerate}[nosep, leftmargin=1.4em]
\item \textbf{Dense $\rightarrow$ sparse reward.} Shaping that specifies
\emph{how} to fly encodes a trajectory manifold that is optimal only under
the training dynamics.
\item \textbf{Exploit tolerance $\rightarrow$ exploit detection.} In
simulation we deliberately removed a termination rule that punished
reverse gate passes, because tolerating the mistake trained more smoothly.
In the transfer phase we added three separate detectors.
\item \textbf{Randomization as insurance $\rightarrow$ randomization as
identified uncertainty.} Widening the distribution around an unidentified
nominal buys robustness in directions that do not matter.
\item \textbf{Training return $\rightarrow$ deployment metric for checkpoint
selection.} The standard \code{best\_model.pt} rule---argmax of mean
training return---selected a checkpoint whose measured three-lap success
rate was $84.7\%$.
\end{enumerate}

The fifth change was not an inversion but a constraint: the observation space
stopped being a free design variable and became an interface contract with
the deployment controller.

Our contributions are empirical rather than algorithmic. We use standard PPO
\cite{schulman2017} with generalized advantage estimation \cite{schulman2016}
throughout, and the learning algorithm is not where the difficulty lay. What
we offer is a measured account of (i) a reward-hacking taxonomy induced by a
self-intersecting task graph and a general fix for it, (ii) a reset
distribution treated as a first-class design object including its interaction
with actuator state, (iii) a sensitivity analysis that reallocated our
randomization budget from dynamics to observations, and (iv) two opposite
failure modes of evaluation-harness design.

\section{Problem Setup}

\subsection{Task and control stack}

The vehicle is a Crazyflie-class quadrotor simulated in Isaac Lab
\cite{mittal2023}. Rigid-body physics runs at $500$\,Hz. The policy acts at
$50$\,Hz (decimation $10$) and emits a four-dimensional continuous action
$a_t = [\,\bar{T}, \omega_x^{\text{des}}, \omega_y^{\text{des}},
\omega_z^{\text{des}}\,] \in [-1,1]^4$: a normalized collective thrust and
three body-rate commands. An inner PID rate controller running at $500$\,Hz
converts the rate commands to a desired wrench, which a first-order motor
model tracks. Episodes last $30$\,s or terminate on crash; the task caps at
three laps.

This action interface is the same one exposed by the physical stack, which is
a precondition for transfer rather than a contribution. It also determines
what \emph{can} be randomized: everything between the action and the resulting
wrench---thrust authority, motor time constant, rate-loop gains, aerodynamic
drag, command latency---is a candidate parameter, and we return to this in
Section~\ref{sec:dr}.

\subsection{Track geometry and its consequences}

