central double-gate structure; waypoint 6 is waypoint 3's plane traversed in
the opposite direction.}
\label{tab:track}
\begin{tabular}{@{}c r r r r@{}}
\toprule
$i$ & $x$ (m) & $y$ (m) & $z$ (m) & yaw (rad) \\
\midrule
0 & $2.000$  & $3.5$  & $0.75$ & $-1.5708$ \\
1 & $-1.500$ & $3.5$  & $2.00$ & $0.7854$ \\
2 & $-0.625$ & $0.0$  & $0.75$ & $1.5708$ \\
3 & $0.625$  & $0.0$  & $0.75$ & $1.5708$ \\
4 & $-1.500$ & $-3.5$ & $2.00$ & $2.3560$ \\
5 & $2.000$  & $-3.5$ & $0.75$ & $-1.5708$ \\
6 & $0.625$  & $0.0$  & $0.75$ & $-1.5708$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:track} lists the course. Six of the seven waypoints are
ordinary; the interesting structure is central. Waypoints 2 and 3 are two
gate planes $1.25$\,m apart at the same altitude and the same heading, and
waypoint 6 is \emph{the same plane as waypoint 3, entered from the opposite
side}. The course therefore self-intersects: the state
$(\text{position}, \text{velocity})$ does not determine which waypoint the
agent is pursuing, and a single physical gate crossing can be a legitimate
pass, an illegitimate reverse pass, or a pass toward a different waypoint
index, depending on history.

Nearly every difficulty in this project traces back to that fact. It makes
gate-pass detection a stateful predicate rather than a geometric test; it
creates a family of reward exploits that shortcut the intended path
(Section~\ref{sec:hacking}); and because the drone must gain and lose
$1.25$\,m of altitude in the shortest segment of the course, it is where
dense shaping and dynamics mismatch both bite hardest.

A gate pass at waypoint $i$ is detected in the gate's local frame: the
agent's $x$-coordinate must cross from positive to negative between
consecutive steps while the $(y,z)$ coordinates lie inside the opening. We
write the half-side of the opening used for this test as $s$, and note here
that the sim-to-real phase splits $s$ into two different values for two
different purposes---a point developed in Section~\ref{sec:hacking}.

\subsection{Evaluation protocols}
\label{sec:protocols}

Because we report numbers from four distinct protocols, we name them once.

\begin{itemize}[nosep, leftmargin=1.2em]
\item \textbf{Self-evaluation (simulation phase).} Batch rollouts across
thousands of parallel environments, each drawing independent perturbations of
thrust-to-weight, aerodynamic drag, and rate-loop gains. Reports three-lap
success rate and time distribution.
\item \textbf{External evaluation (simulation phase).} An independently
specified protocol with fixed perturbation bounds and a reference time of
$16.06$\,s, applied to a submitted policy. We report our own measurements
against its bounds rather than its outputs.
\item \textbf{Batch evaluation (sim-to-real phase).} Reset-diverse rollouts
under the re-identified dynamics nominal, used as the primary robustness
screen.
\item \textbf{Physical evaluation.} Timed three-lap runs on the real vehicle
in a motion-capture arena, with lap timing recovered post hoc from flight
logs under a strict gate-ordering rule.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{These protocols are not mutually comparable, and we do not compare
them.} In particular, simulation-phase times and sim-to-real-phase times
differ by roughly $2.7$\,s not because the policy got worse but because the
two phases run under different dynamics nominals: the simulation phase uses
the simulator's default thrust-to-weight ratio of $3.15$, while the
sim-to-real phase re-centers it on the value identified from flight logs
(Section~\ref{sec:twr}). A policy with less thrust authority is slower; that
is physics, not regression.

\section{Learning Algorithm and Infrastructure}

We use PPO \cite{schulman2017} with the clipped surrogate objective, an
entropy bonus, and GAE \cite{schulman2016} ($\gamma = 0.99$,
$\lambda = 0.95$), training across $8192$--$16384$ parallel environments in
the style of massively parallel on-policy learning \cite{rudin2022}. Three
implementation choices mattered enough to record.

\textbf{KL-adaptive learning rate.} Early prototypes exhibited late-training
policy collapse around $2500$ iterations. We adopted a schedule that
estimates the approximate KL divergence per update and halves the learning
rate when it exceeds $2\bar{d}_{\mathrm{KL}}$ ($\bar{d}_{\mathrm{KL}} =
0.01$), scaling up by $1.5\times$ when it falls below
$\bar{d}_{\mathrm{KL}}/2$, clamped to $[10^{-5}, 10^{-2}]$. This removed the
collapse mode in the simulation phase; it did not prevent the reward-driven
collapses described in Section~\ref{sec:sim-reward}, which were a shaping
problem rather than a step-size problem.

\textbf{Vectorized advantage computation.} GAE was refactored so that all TD
residuals $\delta_t$ are computed in a single batched GPU pass, leaving only
the backward recursion sequential. At $16384$ environments the original
per-step formulation was dominated by kernel launch overhead.

\textbf{Configuration differences between phases.} Table~\ref{tab:ppo} lists
them. Two are consequences of decisions made later in this report and are
worth flagging early. The rollout length increase from $24$ to $64$ steps is
forced by the reward becoming sparse: at $50$\,Hz, a $24$-step rollout spans
$0.48$\,s, which frequently contains no gate-pass event at all, so most
advantage estimates carry no task signal. And \code{empirical\_normalization}
is disabled in the sim-to-real phase not because it hurts but because the
running observation normalizer is a \emph{learned component of the policy}
that the deployment controller never receives. An earlier attempt to
substitute hand-chosen fixed normalization constants failed to recover the
same performance, which we read as evidence that the normalizer's running
statistics were doing real work---work we then had to forgo.

