The deeper critic in the sim-to-real phase follows the observation of
Pasumarti et al.\ \cite{pasumarti2025} that value estimation under sparse
racing objectives benefits from more capacity than the policy needs; the
actor width is instead fixed by the deployment controller's network
definition.

\section{Simulation Phase}

\subsection{Observation space}

The simulation-phase policy receives a $31$-dimensional ego-centric
observation, all spatial quantities in the body frame: body linear and
angular velocity ($6$); the gravity vector projected into the body frame
($3$); current, next, and next-next gate positions ($9$); entry-direction
normals for the current and next gates ($6$); the normalized waypoint index
($1$); $\sin$ and $\cos$ of the yaw error to the target gate ($2$); and the
previous action ($4$).

Two choices are worth isolating. Replacing the unit quaternion with the
body-frame gravity vector removes a dimension while preserving all tilt
information the task needs, and eliminates the double-cover sign ambiguity.
The normalized waypoint index is a deliberate concession to the track's
self-intersection: since position and velocity do not identify which waypoint
is active, the index restores the Markov property that the geometry destroys.
Without it the policy cannot in principle distinguish waypoint 3 from
waypoint 6.

\subsection{Reward and the collapse modes it produced}
\label{sec:sim-reward}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Simulation-phase reward terms.}
\label{tab:sim-reward}
\begin{tabular}{@{}l r p{3.5cm}@{}}
\toprule
Term & Scale & Definition \\
\midrule
Gate pass       & $200.0$ & Indicator of a valid pass \\
Progress        & $50.0$  & $\Delta$ distance to target, clamped $[-1,1]$ \\
Racing-line vel.& $8.0$   & Blended velocity projection, clamped $[-2,8]$ \\
Orientation     & $-1.0$  & $|\phi| + |\theta|$ when $>0.5$\,rad \\
Smoothness      & $-0.2$  & $\|a_t - a_{t-1}\|_2$ \\
Contact         & $-1.0$  & Per-step contact indicator \\
Termination     & $-10.0$ & Replaces the reward on death \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:sim-reward} gives the converged configuration. It is the
result of a search whose failures are more informative than its endpoint.

Attempts to add an explicit heading-alignment term ($\cos$ of yaw error,
scale $0.5$--$2.0$) consistently degraded performance, and did so in a
diagnosable way. Combined with a velocity reward at scale $5.0$, whose
episodic contribution reached ${\sim}750$ against the gate-pass term's
${\sim}200$, the policy became speed-dominated: it accelerated toward each
gate at maximum rate and could not decelerate for the tight transition out of
the central structure. Gate-pass reward collapsed from ${\sim}200$ to
${\sim}40$ while velocity reward rose to ${\sim}150$. The lesson is a
quantitative one about shaping: the relevant comparison is not between reward
\emph{scales} but between their \emph{integrated episodic contributions},
and a dense term integrated over a $30$\,s episode can silently dominate a
sparse term worth $200$ per event.

Relaxing the orientation penalty produced a different failure. At scale
$-0.5$ with a $1.0$\,rad threshold, the policy completed laps but developed a
tumbling recovery at the exit of the central structure. At $-2.0$ with a
$0.5$\,rad threshold it was stable but slow. The converged $-1.0$ at
$0.5$\,rad permits aggressive banking while penalizing attitudes from which
the rate controller cannot recover.

\subsection{Shaping as implicit path planning}

Rather than specifying a geometric racing line, we shaped the velocity reward
to induce one. Let $\hat{d}_{\text{cur}}$ and $\hat{d}_{\text{next}}$ be unit
vectors from the vehicle to the current target and to the following gate. The
velocity term rewards
\begin{equation}
r_{\text{vel}} \propto
\alpha \, \langle v, \hat{d}_{\text{cur}} \rangle +
(1-\alpha) \, \langle v, \hat{d}_{\text{next}} \rangle ,
\end{equation}
with $\alpha = 5/8$ on ordinary gates. Because part of the reward points at
the \emph{next} gate before the current one is reached, the optimal
trajectory cuts toward the inside of each corner. This produced smooth
curving racing lines with no path parameterization, no spline, and no tuning
per corner.

The blend is a genuine optimization knob with a stability boundary. Reducing
$\alpha$ to $4/8$---an apparently mild change---caused a late-training
collapse at approximately $3000$ iterations, with three-lap success dropping
from ${\sim}90\%$ to zero and mean lap time spiking to ${\sim}20$\,s before
partially recovering to a degraded plateau. The next-gate component pulls the
vehicle far enough off the current gate's approach at high speed to cause
misses, and once misses become common the sparse gate reward degrades faster
than the dense velocity term, which is a self-reinforcing dynamic. We report
this because $\alpha$ looks like a hyperparameter and behaves like a phase
boundary.

At the central structure ($i=3$) the blend is disabled and the term uses the
current target only, for reasons given next.

\subsection{Virtual targets}

The gate-pass predicate is computed from the vehicle's pose in the gate
frame, and is therefore independent of whatever position the reward function
happens to be aiming at. This decoupling---a target variable
\code{desired\_pos\_w} distinct from the gate itself---lets us steer shaping
without altering the task definition, and we exploited it heavily.

The central structure was designed to force a vertical loop. We found that a
path around the \emph{side} of the structure was faster and implemented it as
a two-phase virtual target. While pursuing waypoint 2, the target is
overridden to an apex at $[0, -0.3, 1.6]$, which pulls the vehicle upward
early; it clips through waypoint 2's opening while already climbing, without
ever aiming at that gate's center. While pursuing waypoint 3, the target
remains the apex until either $z > 1.3$\,m or the distance to the apex falls
below $0.8$\,m, at which point it switches to an offset point at
$[0.425, 0, 0.75]$, producing a dive past the side of the structure and
straight through the opening. An earlier three-phase variant with an
intermediate pre-entry waypoint was slower and was removed.

This technique is powerful and does not survive contact with hardware. It
encodes a specific solution to a specific geometry under specific dynamics,
which is exactly the property we later had to give up.

\subsection{Reset distribution}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Simulation-phase reset distribution.}
\label{tab:sim-reset}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{2.1cm} p{5.3cm}@{}}
\toprule
Component & Specification \\
\midrule
Target waypoint & Uniform over all seven, so every segment trains equally. \\
\addlinespace
Spawn pose & Behind the target at a distance ramping $[1.0,2.0] \rightarrow [1.5,4.0]$\,m over $800$ iterations; lateral $\pm1.0$\,m, vertical $\pm0.5$\,m, yaw $\pm0.3$\,rad; altitude clamped above $0.15$\,m. \\
\addlinespace
Mid-segment ($40\%$) & Uniform at $20$--$80\%$ of the way between consecutive gates. \\
\addlinespace
Ground (${\sim}20\%$) & Two independent ${\sim}10\%$ mechanisms placing the vehicle at $z=0.05$\,m, one retaining velocity and one at rest. \\
\addlinespace
Velocity init.\ ($50\%$) & Half of airborne spawns receive $0.5$--$3.0$\,m/s of forward velocity. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:sim-reset} gives the distribution. One entry deserves
emphasis: raising the mid-segment spawn rate from $25\%$ to $40\%$ moved
three-lap success from $86.7\%$ to $96.3\%$. This was the single largest
measured improvement of the simulation phase, larger than any reward change,
and it was obtained by altering where episodes begin rather than what they
reward.

The mechanism is straightforward once stated. On-policy methods fit the value
function only on the state distribution induced by the initial-state
distribution $\rho_0$ and the current policy $\pi$. Spawning behind gates
trains gate approaches well and gate-to-gate \emph{transitions} poorly,
because transition states are visited only by trajectories that have already
succeeded at everything preceding them. Mid-segment spawning removes that
dependency. We return to this idea in Section~\ref{sec:reset}, where it
becomes a design object in its own right.

\subsection{Randomization and fine-tuning}

Physical parameters---thrust-to-weight, aerodynamic drag coefficients, and
rate-loop PID gains---were resampled per environment at every reset,
following standard dynamics randomization practice
\cite{tobin2017,peng2018}. The final simulation-phase model was produced in
two stages: a baseline trained for $5000$ iterations at $16384$ environments,
then fine-tuned for a further $2000$ iterations under deliberately widened
bounds (thrust-to-weight $\pm8\%$, drag $0.3$--$2.5\times$, PID $\pm25\%$ on
$k_p, k_i$ and $\pm40\%$ on $k_d$) with the entropy coefficient reduced from
$0.005$ to $0.001$ to sharpen the policy.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Paired evaluation over $1000$ environments with identical
perturbation draws, bucketed by baseline performance.}
\label{tab:paired}
\begin{tabular}{@{}l r@{}}
\toprule
Baseline bucket & Fine-tuned $\Delta$ time \\
\midrule
$<15.6$\,s (baseline's best)      & $+0.87$\,s (worse) \\
$15.9$--$16.1$\,s (borderline)    & $-1.32$\,s \\
$16.1$--$16.4$\,s (at risk)       & $-0.99$\,s \\
$\geq 16.4$\,s (baseline's worst) & $-1.38$\,s \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The comparison we found most instructive was a \emph{paired} evaluation:
$1000$ environments with identical perturbation draws presented to both
models. Rank correlation between the two models' per-environment times was
essentially zero (Pearson $r = -0.016$, Spearman $\rho = 0.048$).
Fine-tuning under wider bounds did not translate the performance
distribution; it \emph{rotated} it. Table~\ref{tab:paired} shows the
structure: the fine-tuned model is worse exactly where the baseline was best
and better everywhere else, converting $168$ environments---$78.5\%$ of the
baseline's failures---into passes while introducing $137$ new ones, for a net
gain of $31$.

This is worth stating as a general caution. A headline success-rate
improvement of $2.4$ percentage points concealed $305$ environments whose
outcome changed, in both directions. Aggregate metrics on independently
randomized environments cannot distinguish a uniform improvement from a
reshuffle, and the two have very different implications for deployment: a
reshuffle means the new model has new blind spots that the old model's
evaluation history says nothing about.

\subsection{Simulation-phase results}

Under self-evaluation across $5000$ independently randomized environments,
the fine-tuned model achieved $97.3\%$ three-lap success at a mean time of
$15.68$\,s (median $15.58$\,s, $\sigma = 0.50$\,s, best $14.82$\,s, worst
$21.26$\,s), against the baseline's $94.9\%$ and $15.78$\,s
($\sigma = 0.54$\,s). Measured against the external protocol's $16.06$\,s
reference, $84.0\%$ of environments finished inside the reference time,
against $82.0\%$ for the baseline.

\section{The Sim-to-Real Phase: Interface Constraints}

Transfer changed what was adjustable before it changed what was optimal.

\subsection{The observation space becomes a contract}

The deployment controller computes its own observation vector from
motion-capture data. Any quantity the policy consumes must be reproducible by
that code, which eliminated most of the simulation-phase observation design.
Engineered features---yaw error, normalized waypoint index, gate
normals---were replaced by raw geometry:
\begin{equation*}
\underbrace{v_b}_{3} \;\big|\;
\underbrace{\mathrm{vec}(R_{b \rightarrow w})}_{9} \;\big|\;
\underbrace{C^{\text{cur}}_b}_{12} \;\big|\;
\underbrace{C^{\text{next}}_b}_{12} \;\big|\;
\underbrace{a_{t-1}}_{4}
\end{equation*}
for a total of $40$ dimensions, where $C_b$ denotes the four corners of a
gate expressed in the body frame, computed identically on both sides as
$C_w = S R_g^\top + p_g$ followed by $C_b = (C_w - p_d) R_b$.

Two remarks. First, the full rotation matrix replaces the gravity vector:
having given up the yaw-error feature, the policy needs complete attitude,
and a rotation matrix is a continuous representation without the sign
ambiguity of quaternions. Second, twelve numbers per gate is a redundant
encoding of a four-degree-of-freedom object, and we adopted it not because it
is efficient but because it is what the deployment controller already
computed. \emph{A feature that cannot be reproduced by the deployment stack
is not available at any price}, and feature engineering that helps in
simulation becomes a liability once the feature pipeline must exist twice and
agree.

Including the previous action follows Kaufmann et al.\ \cite{kaufmann2023}:
under actuator lag, the previous command carries information about the
current actuator state that the kinematic observation does not.

\section{Sparse Reward Redesign}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Sim-to-real-phase reward terms. Dense progress remains available but
is disabled by default.}
\label{tab:s2r-reward}
\begin{tabular}{@{}l r p{3.3cm}@{}}
\toprule
Term & Scale & Definition \\
\midrule
Gate pass        & $200.0$ & Indicator, inner opening \\
Lap incomplete   & $-0.05$ & Constant, per step \\
Rate reg.\ (r/p) & $-1.0$  & $\omega_x^2 + \omega_y^2$ (commanded) \\
Rate reg.\ (yaw) & $-0.5$  & $\omega_z^2$ (commanded) \\
Action smoothness& $-0.1$  & $\|a_t - a_{t-1}\|_2^2$ \\
Contact          & --      & Per-step contact indicator \\
Termination      & --      & Replaces the reward on death \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:s2r-reward} gives the redesigned reward. Progress, velocity
blending, orientation penalty, and the virtual-target machinery were all
removed. The argument, following the sparse-objective results of
\cite{pasumarti2025} and the general observation that shaping alters the
optimal policy unless it is potential-based \cite{ng1999}, is that dense
terms specify \emph{how} to fly and thereby encode a trajectory manifold that
is optimal only under the training dynamics. When those dynamics shift, the
policy has been given no reason to have learned anything off that manifold.
A sparse objective specifies \emph{what} to achieve and leaves the solution
free to be re-derived.

Two results from this redesign generalize beyond our setting.

\textbf{Sparse bonuses must beat integrated, not instantaneous, costs.} Our
first sparse configuration set the gate-pass bonus to $10.0$ and collapsed at
approximately $200$ iterations: the policy learned to hover. With a constant
per-step penalty and a per-step contact penalty always active, and a gate
requiring on the order of a second to reach, the expected integrated cost of
\emph{attempting} a gate exceeded the bonus for succeeding. Raising the bonus
to $200.0$ fixed it. The design condition is that a sparse bonus must exceed
the integrated negative cost accumulated over the expected time to earn it,
which for a $30$\,s episode at $50$\,Hz is a factor of hundreds away from the
instantaneous comparison one is tempted to make.

\textbf{Command regularization is a calibrated speed dial.} We ablated the
roll/pitch and yaw rate penalties from $(-1.0, -0.5)$ to $(-2.0, -0.05)$,
motivated by the observation that in flight logs the yaw rate stays low
throughout while roll and pitch drive the aggressive transients---so the
default split under-penalized the axis that produced jerk and
over-penalized one that did not. The rebalanced policy was the smoothest we
trained, with the lowest mean tilt and mean body rate of any policy we flew,
and it was reliably slower by roughly $0.6$\,s per lap. This is an exchange
rate, not a failure: the term buys attitude margin at a measurable price in
time, and it is the correct knob when margin is the binding constraint.

\section{Specification Gaming on a Self-Intersecting Task Graph}
\label{sec:hacking}

Because waypoints 2, 3 and 6 share the central structure and waypoint 6
reuses waypoint 3's plane in reverse, a naive gate-pass predicate admits
several ways to earn credit without flying the intended course. We
encountered three, each requiring a different fix.

\textbf{Reverse crossing of the current target.} The vehicle crosses its
target gate's plane in the wrong direction. Detected by the mirrored
predicate (negative to positive $x$ within bounds) and treated as
termination. A five-step cooldown after a legitimate pass is required, since
the frame recomputation at the moment of passing otherwise produces false
positives.

\textbf{Plane-straddling at the loop gate.} A subtler exploit: the vehicle
approaches waypoint 3, crosses the plane by a few centimetres so that
$x_{t-1} > 0$ and $x_t \leq 0$, and immediately reverses out. It collects the
pass bonus without traversing the structure. The fix conditions the pass on
having genuinely been in the approach zone: we track
$\max_{\tau \leq t} x_\tau$ since the target last changed and require it to
exceed a threshold. Critically, we apply this \emph{only} at waypoint 3.
Applying it globally caused visibly valid passes elsewhere on the course to
go uncounted, which corrupted our own evaluation.

\textbf{Backtracking through the previous gate.} After passing waypoint 2 the
vehicle reverses back through waypoint 2's plane to re-enter the $+x$
approach region, then proceeds to waypoint 3---a cheap way to restart the
approach without a proper detour, available precisely because waypoints 2 and
3 share a structure and a heading. The existing checks did not see it: they
monitor only the \emph{current} target, and once waypoint 2 was passed
nothing in the reward function cared about its plane any longer. The fix is a
generic previous-gate detector, computing the vehicle's position in the frame
of waypoint $(i-1) \bmod 7$ each step and terminating on a
negative-to-positive crossing within bounds. It requires three guards: skip
while no gate has yet been passed, skip on the step a gate is passed (the
previous-gate index has just changed, making the buffered comparison
meaningless), and test only within the opening, so that flying around the
outside of a passed gate never triggers.

\subsection{Splitting the reward predicate from the progress predicate}

The three fixes above are patches. The structural change that made them
coherent was to stop using one predicate for two purposes.

Our earlier configurations used a single gate half-side $s$ for everything.
Shrinking $s$ to encourage conservative center-line flight therefore also
shrank the region in which the agent's \emph{target advanced}, producing
episodes where the vehicle physically flew through a gate and the task did
not acknowledge it. That is not conservatism; it is a corrupted task
definition.

We now maintain two predicates over the same crossing event:
\begin{itemize}[nosep, leftmargin=1.2em]
\item a \textbf{progress predicate} at the physical half-side ($0.5$\,m),
which advances the target index, increments the lap counter, updates the
desired position, and drives termination logic; and
\item a \textbf{reward predicate} at a virtual inner half-side
($s/2 = 0.35$\,m), which alone controls the gate-pass bonus and insertion
into the replay buffer described in Section~\ref{sec:reset}.
\end{itemize}

The agent is thus paid for flying through the middle while the task counts
what physically happened. We regard this as the most portable idea in this
report: whenever a shaping term and a task-progress condition are computed
from the same geometric test, tightening the shaping silently redefines the
task, and the two should be separated at the outset.

A related instance: we added the option to zero the dense progress term at
selected waypoints, used at the loop gate, because Euclidean distance to a
gate center shrinks as the vehicle approaches from the \emph{wrong} side as
readily as from the right one. Dense progress rewards are not
direction-aware, and on a self-intersecting course that is exploitable.

\subsection{The same exploit, two correct answers}

In the simulation phase we experimented with strict termination on reverse
and wrong-side passes and \emph{deliberately removed it}. Allowing the agent
to make the mistake without instantly ending the episode produced noticeably
smoother and more stable training. In the sim-to-real phase we added three
detectors.

Both decisions were right for their regime, and the difference is what the
exploit costs. Where the simulator defines correctness, an exploit costs a
suboptimal trajectory, and the termination that suppresses it costs
gradient signal and training stability---frequently a bad trade. Where the
simulator stands in for hardware, an exploit costs a policy that has learned
a behavior the real geometry will not support, discovered at deployment. The
same reward-hacking observation therefore licenses opposite interventions
depending on whether simulator fidelity is in question, and we think this is
under-appreciated in discussions of specification gaming
\cite{amodei2016}, which typically treat exploits as unconditionally
undesirable.

\section{The Reset Distribution as a Design Object}
\label{sec:reset}

Having found in the simulation phase that reset placement outweighed reward
tuning, we treated $\rho_0$ explicitly in the transfer phase. The
distribution is a mixture of four components.

\textbf{Gate-pass replay ($30\%$ nominal).} Following the practice of
resetting to states observed during successful task execution
\cite{kaufmann2023,song2023}, we maintain a per-waypoint ring buffer keyed by
the \emph{next} target gate. Whenever the reward predicate fires, the full
root state (position, orientation, linear and angular velocity) and the
previous action are written to the buffer for the newly acquired target. At
reset, a state is drawn and perturbed: $\pm0.15$\,m horizontally, $\pm0.10$\,m
vertically (clamped above $0.15$\,m), $\pm0.20$\,m/s linear velocity,
$\pm0.50$\,rad/s angular velocity, $\pm0.20$\,rad yaw. The buffer contains
only states the policy actually reached, so the distribution tracks the
policy as it improves.

\textbf{Actuator-state reconstruction.} A detail we consider necessary rather
than optional: replaying a rigid-body state into a system with actuator
dynamics does not replay the state. The rotor speeds at the recorded instant
were not at rest, and initializing them to zero injects a spin-up transient
into precisely the states we selected for being important. We therefore
recompute the desired wrench from the replayed previous action, invert the
mixer to obtain motor-speed targets, and initialize both the commanded and
actual motor speeds to that value, so that the first step after reset is
dynamically continuous with the trajectory the state was drawn from.

\textbf{Ground starts ($20\%$ nominal).} The physical evaluation begins with
the vehicle at rest on the floor, so a fraction of episodes start at
$z \in [0.03, 0.06]$\,m behind the first gate, at rest, with
$x \in [-3.0, -0.5]$\,m and $y \in [-1.0, 1.0]$\,m in the gate's local frame
and $\pm0.15$\,rad of yaw noise. A narrowed variant restricts this to
$x \in [-2.2,-1.2]$\,m, $y \in [-0.25, 0.25]$\,m and $\pm0.08$\,rad, matching
the observed launch pose in the arena.

\textbf{Geometric fallback.} Remaining episodes interpolate between
consecutive gates with $t \sim \mathrm{Beta}(0.5, 0.5)$, which concentrates
mass near gates and thins it mid-segment, plus $\pm0.3$\,m lateral and
$\pm0.2$\,m vertical noise perpendicular to the segment.

We additionally implemented staged ramping of the replay fraction (zero
before a warmup iteration, linear to full thereafter), on the reasoning that
an empty or unrepresentative buffer early in training supplies states the
policy cannot yet handle.

\subsection{A cautionary ablation}

The geometric fallback spawns at rest, which is unlike race flight, so we
added an option to give it velocity toward the target. At a cap of
$1.2$\,m/s the policy became measurably faster but lost success rate and grew
a worse time tail; at $0.6$\,m/s success rate recovered ($98.3\%$) but the
speed gain vanished entirely (mean $19.46$\,s, against $18.34$\,s for the
base). We had framed this as distribution matching. The result says
otherwise: rather than making training states more realistic, the higher
reset velocity trained a more aggressive state distribution, buying speed by
shifting the policy rather than by correcting a mismatch. The distinction
matters, and success rate at fixed mean time is what separates them.

\section{Randomization as Identified Uncertainty}
\label{sec:dr}

\subsection{Available parameters}

The transfer-phase environment exposes per-environment randomization of the
transition model---thrust-to-weight, aerodynamic drag, rate-loop $k_p, k_i,
k_d$, vehicle mass, motor time constant, and action latency (0--2 policy
steps, sampled or held fixed)---and of the observation map: per-channel
Gaussian noise (default $\sigma = 0.05$\,m/s on body velocity, $0.01$ on
rotation-matrix entries, $0.02$\,m on gate corners, none on the previous
action), observation latency, and systematic biases on body-frame velocity,
gate-corner position, and yaw. All are resampled at reset. The distinction
between the two groups turned out to matter more than any individual range.

\subsection{Sensitivity analysis as budget allocation}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Deterministic robustness sweep, $768$ ground-start episodes per
scenario, single checkpoint. Takeoff and first-gate success were $100\%$ in
all thirteen scenarios; the separating signal is entirely in later trajectory
execution.}
\label{tab:sweep}
\begin{tabular}{@{}l l r r r@{}}
\toprule
Scenario & Group & 3-lap SR (\%) & Mean 3-lap (s) & $\Delta$SR (pp) \\
\midrule
\code{control\_nominal}        & control & $96.6$ & $18.65$ & $+0.0$ \\
\code{control\_thrust\_0p90}   & control & $100.0$ & $18.33$ & $+3.4$ \\
\code{control\_thrust\_1p10}   & control & $96.2$ & $18.64$ & $-0.4$ \\
\code{control\_latency\_20ms}  & control & $96.9$ & $18.40$ & $+0.3$ \\
\code{control\_latency\_40ms}  & control & $99.9$ & $18.41$ & $+3.3$ \\
\code{control\_rate\_gain\_0p85} & control & $95.6$ & $18.65$ & $-1.0$ \\
\code{control\_rate\_gain\_1p15} & control & $99.5$ & $18.38$ & $+2.9$ \\
\midrule
\code{obs\_vel\_noise\_0p05}   & observation & $96.5$ & $18.81$ & $-0.1$ \\
\code{obs\_yaw\_bias\_5deg}    & observation & $99.9$ & $18.30$ & $+3.3$ \\
\code{obs\_delay\_20ms}        & observation & $98.2$ & $18.40$ & $+1.6$ \\
\code{obs\_delay\_40ms}        & observation & $100.0$ & $18.39$ & $+3.4$ \\
\code{obs\_gate\_bias\_5cm}    & observation & $92.4$ & $20.76$ & $-4.2$ \\
\textbf{\code{obs\_vel\_bias\_0p20}} & observation & $\mathbf{2.9}$ & $\mathbf{28.15}$ & $\mathbf{-93.8}$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

Before committing randomization budget we ran a deterministic, evaluation-only
sweep over thirteen perturbation scenarios, $768$ ground-start episodes each,
on a single checkpoint (Table~\ref{tab:sweep}). The question was narrow: is
this policy more likely to fail from moderate control mismatch or from
systematic observation mismatch?

The answer was unambiguous. Every transition-model perturbation we tested
left three-lap success at or above $95.6\%$, including a $10\%$ thrust error,
a $40$\,ms command latency, and $\pm15\%$ rate-loop gain error. Zero-mean
observation noise at the trained magnitude was likewise harmless
($96.5\%$). But a constant $0.20$\,m/s bias on the observed body-frame
velocity reduced success to $2.9\%$, and a $5$\,cm systematic offset on gate
corners cost $4.2$ points and $2.1$\,s.

The interpretation we favor is that a closed loop rejects transition-model
error as a matter of course---that is what feedback is for---whereas a
systematic observation bias is an unmodeled, unobservable state offset that
the policy cannot detect and has no mechanism to correct. Randomizing
dynamics teaches robustness to a disturbance the controller was already going
to reject; randomizing observation \emph{bias} teaches robustness to
something it cannot.

Two boundaries on this claim. It is a sim-side sensitivity result for one
checkpoint, not evidence that the physical system carries a $0.20$\,m/s
velocity bias. And several control scenarios scored slightly \emph{above}
nominal, which is within run-to-run variation and should not be read as a
trend. What survives is the ordering: for this policy class, systematic
observation bias is the first-order risk and moderate actuator mismatch is
not.

\subsection{Re-centering the transition model}
\label{sec:twr}

Randomization defends against parameters near the center of its distribution.
It cannot defend against a center that is wrong.

Analysis of flight logs indicated an effective vehicle mass near $64$\,g,
against the simulator's nominal $30$\,g, while the deployed controller maps
full policy thrust to approximately $1.174$\,N. The implied effective
thrust-to-weight ratio is
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{TWR}_{\text{real}} \approx
\frac{1.174\ \mathrm{N}}{0.064\ \mathrm{kg} \times 9.81\ \mathrm{m/s^2}}
\approx 1.87 ,
\end{equation}
against a simulator nominal of $3.15$. Our widest randomization to that point
was $\pm8\%$, spanning $[2.90, 3.40]$. \textbf{The physical operating point
was not in a low-density tail of the training distribution; it was outside
its support entirely.} No width of randomization around $3.15$ would have
reached it, and no reward, architecture, or reset change could have
compensated: the policy was learning to exploit control authority the vehicle
did not have.

Re-centering the nominal on $1.87$ with a narrow $\pm6\%$ band produced
$98.0\%$ three-lap success at $18.34$\,s ($\sigma = 0.48$\,s) over $768$
environments. Notably, an \emph{untrained-for} sanity check---running the old
checkpoint under the new nominal---already completed three laps, at
$21.16$\,s, which is what gave us confidence the direction was right before
committing to a full retrain.

The cost is specialization. The re-centered policy performs poorly at the
original nominal, terminating around seven gates on representative rollouts.
This is the robustness--specialization trade documented by Ferede et al.\
\cite{ferede2025}: less randomization transfers faster when the nominal is
correct, and fails outright when it is not. We accepted the trade because the
deployment thrust scale is fixed and known, but it means the resulting policy
is a policy for \emph{this vehicle}, not a policy for quadrotors.

Two general statements follow. First, randomization widens support; it does
not move the center, and the two are not interchangeable. Second, the cheapest
diagnostic for a suspected support failure is not a wider sweep but a single
rollout at the hypothesized nominal.

\subsection{Randomization width after identification}

With the nominal corrected we tested whether tightening the remaining ranges
(thrust-to-weight $\pm4\%$, drag $0.8$--$1.2\times$, PID $\pm8\%/\pm15\%$)
would buy speed. Across four candidates it did not: the best tight-bound run
reached $98.3\%$ at $19.46$\,s, slower than the base. We read this as
evidence that once the nominal is right, the residual gap for this policy
family is geometric---racing line and clearance---rather than a
randomization-width effect, and that further width tuning was not the correct
tool. Action-latency randomization was retained regardless, for reasons
Section~\ref{sec:eval} makes clear.

\section{Evaluation and Policy Selection}
\label{sec:eval}

Three separate evaluation-design failures cost us more than any modeling
error, and we believe they generalize.

\subsection{Training return is not the deployment objective}

The training framework saves \code{best\_model.pt} at the argmax of mean
episodic training return. Nothing about that quantity optimizes what
deployment requires: three-lap success rate, low variance, and a bounded
worst case. The gap is not academic. In one run the saved best model was
written at approximately iteration $513$ at a mean training return of
$5746.86$; its batch evaluation was $84.7\%$ success, $19.09$\,s mean,
$1.26$\,s standard deviation, and a $28.44$\,s worst case---a checkpoint that
wins on return by flying fast and occasionally failing badly, which is
exactly the profile training return cannot distinguish from a good one.

We replaced the rule with an explicit selection procedure: assemble a
candidate pool (the saved best model plus the highest-return checkpoints
across the run, plus a few late-training anchors so that a single reward
spike cannot monopolize the pool), evaluate every candidate under the
deployment-matched protocol, and accept only if success rate
$\geq 98.5\%$, mean time $\leq 18.65$\,s, standard deviation $\leq 0.5$\,s,
and no pathological tail. \textbf{A faster best case is never sufficient
evidence}---a rule we adopted only after a candidate that won on best-case
time failed in the arena.

\subsection{Failure one: an evaluation distribution deployment never samples}

Our batch evaluation runs the environment with training-mode hooks active so
that the dynamics and reset machinery behave normally. A consequence we did
not notice for some time is that it therefore inherits the \emph{training}
reset mixture. With a ground-start fraction of $0.2$, a nominally
$1000$-environment evaluation contained roughly $210$ ground starts; the
remaining $790$ began mid-course, at altitude, with the buffered state of a
policy that had already succeeded. Every physical run begins on the floor at
rest.

The fix is a deployment-matched profile forcing the ground-start fraction to
unity, reported alongside a distinct ground-start success rate. We also
learned to write these results to separate directories, because the most
dangerous artifact of an evaluation bug is a stale result file that silently
answers a question you have since changed.

\subsection{Failure two: an evaluation calibrated past the point of usefulness}

Having identified real parameters, the natural next step was an evaluation
profile that used them: identified thrust-to-weight, a fixed three-step
action delay matching the measured $60$--$70$\,ms command lag, ground starts
only, the narrow launch-pose distribution, and observation noise reduced from
the training value of $0.05$\,m/s to the measured stationary
$0.005$\,m/s. Since a physical session is one vehicle on one day, this seemed
strictly more faithful than randomizing broadly.

We backtested it against three policies whose real-world ranking we already
knew. The result is in Table~\ref{tab:backtest}. The calibrated profile
ranked \emph{first} the policy that in the arena completed a single correctly
ordered lap with negative clearance at a gate, and gave all three policies
$100\%$ success with standard deviations under $0.1$\,s. A latency stress
variant exposed fragility but over-penalized a policy that had completed four
ordered laps. Meanwhile the older, less faithful, reset-diverse screen
correctly kept the base policy first.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Backtest of evaluation profiles against known physical outcomes.
Real ranking: base $>$ speed-v4 $>$ speed-v2.}
\label{tab:backtest}
\begin{tabular}{@{}l r r r@{}}
\toprule
Policy & Calib.\ & Latency & Reset-diverse \\
       & mean (s) & stress SR & SR / mean (s) \\
\midrule
base     & $18.24$ & $100.0\%$ & $98.0\%$ / $18.34$ \\
speed-v2 & $\mathbf{17.96}$ & $45.7\%$ & $96.2\%$ / $18.68$ \\
speed-v4 & $18.57$ & $15.2\%$ & $98.0\%$ / $18.80$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The lesson is not that calibration is wrong but that an evaluation serves two
distinct purposes---estimating performance and \emph{discriminating between
candidates}---and calibrating away the diversity destroys the second. The
perturbations that a faithful single-vehicle profile removes are exactly the
ones under which a fragile policy separates from a robust one. We now require
agreement between the reset-diverse screen, targeted stress checks, and
physical logs before promoting a policy, and treat the calibrated profile as
a diagnostic slice only.

We note the honest limit of this conclusion: the reset-diverse screen did not
predict the speed-v2 failure either. It flagged lower success and much larger
variance, which was enough to rank correctly, but the underlying failure mode
was gate clearance, and no fixed-parameter profile of any calibration can
surface a clearance problem. That remains open.

