\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

Table~\ref{tab:speed} summarizes five attempts to recover speed by reward
modification, all under fixed dynamics and reset settings. None improved mean
time at matched success rate. Two observations. First, the candidate with the
fastest single rollout ($17.44$\,s) had the second-worst variance and failed
in the arena---which is the empirical basis for the selection rule in
Section~\ref{sec:eval}. Second, reintroducing a velocity term narrowly and
weakly (speed-v3, speed-v4) produced stable but slower policies, suggesting
that the dense-to-sparse decision is not finely tunable in this setting: a
small amount of dense shaping bought neither the speed of the simulation-phase
configuration nor an improvement over none at all.

\subsection{Physical evaluation}

The deployed policy completed the timed three-lap evaluation in
$\mathbf{19.004}$\,s and continued to five correctly ordered laps at a mean
lap time of $6.236$\,s, with lap timing recovered from flight logs under a
strict gate-ordering rule that rejects laps containing skipped or misordered
gates. Against the deployment-matched simulation prediction of $18.10$\,s,
the residual gap is approximately $0.9$\,s, or $5\%$.

We do not have an explanation for the residual, and we want to be precise
about why. The three candidate mechanisms---gate clearance margins, gate
geometry calibration, and rate-loop overshoot not captured by our first-order
motor model---are all consistent with the sensitivity analysis, which found
gate-corner bias to be the second most damaging perturbation we tested. But
Section~\ref{sec:eval} establishes that a fixed-parameter evaluation profile
cannot discriminate among them, and we did not have the open-loop excitation
data needed to identify the rate loop. The gap is open.

The two alternative policies flown in the same session ranked in the order
predicted by the reset-diverse screen and against the order predicted by the
calibrated profile, which is the evidence behind Table~\ref{tab:backtest}.

\section{Discussion}

The recurring pattern across both phases is that the components most
responsible for outcomes were not the ones the literature foregrounds. The
learning algorithm was standard and never a bottleneck. Network capacity was
set by an interface constraint rather than by a study. The reward function
mattered, but mostly through a handful of near-discontinuous thresholds---a
sparse bonus that must exceed an integrated cost, a blending coefficient with
a stability boundary---rather than through smooth tuning.

What repeatedly determined outcomes were three objects usually left
implicit:

\textbf{The relationship between the reward predicate and the progress
predicate.} On a course whose task graph self-intersects, a single geometric
test cannot serve both. Conflating them meant that every attempt to make the
policy more conservative also redefined what counted as progress.

\textbf{The initial-state distribution.} The largest single improvement in
the simulation phase came from changing where episodes start, not what they
reward. In the transfer phase, the replay buffer's construction---including
the actuator-state reconstruction most descriptions omit---determined which
regions of the state space carried reliable value estimates.

\textbf{The evaluation harness.} It failed in both directions. Made more
diverse than deployment, it measured a task nobody would run. Calibrated to
match deployment exactly, it lost the ability to discriminate between
candidates and ranked a policy first that failed in the arena. We now think
of these as different instruments with different purposes and refuse to use
either alone.

On randomization specifically, our experience supports a narrower
prescription than the standard one. Identify the nominal first; verify it
with a single rollout at the hypothesized value before committing training
time; then randomize narrowly, and spend the budget where a sensitivity
analysis says the policy is actually fragile. For us that was the observation
map, not the transition model---the opposite of where dynamics-randomization
practice \cite{tobin2017,peng2018} directs attention, and, we suspect, a
consequence of using motion capture, which removes the perception gap and
leaves only a small, systematic, and easily overlooked pipeline bias in its
place \cite{kaufmann2023}.

\section{Limitations and Future Work}

Our identification of the transition model rests on closed-loop race logs,
which are policy-biased and not persistently exciting. They constrain
effective thrust authority and command latency adequately and say very little
about motor time constant, aerodynamic drag, or rate-loop bandwidth. The
appropriate remedy is a dedicated open-loop identification flight with a
phase-structured, time-budgeted excitation schedule---static and hover phases
for mass and observation bias, vertical chirps for the thrust curve and motor
lag, per-axis rate chirps for bandwidth, straight-line segments for drag and
for velocity-estimator lag, and gate flybys to measure gate-corner
calibration error directly. We designed this protocol but did not fly it.

The sensitivity analysis identified observation bias as the dominant risk but
we never measured the real bias, so the corresponding randomization ranges
are hypotheses. The most direct next experiment is to measure stationary
velocity bias and gate-corner calibration error, randomize over the measured
uncertainty, and re-run the sweep with the elimination of the $2.9\%$ collapse
as an explicit go/no-go criterion.

We deliberately did not pursue residual dynamics learning
\cite{kaufmann2023}, in which a learned model corrects simulator error from
real transitions. With the nominal misidentified by a factor approaching two,
a residual model would have been asked to absorb a gross parametric error,
which is both harder to fit and much harder to debug than correcting the
parameter. Once identification is complete, residual learning becomes the
natural next step, and the criterion for needing it is concrete: retrain under
identified randomization, overlay simulated and real state-distribution
envelopes for velocity, tilt, and body rate, and invoke a residual model only
where real coverage exceeds simulated coverage on a specific axis and the
discrepancy has structure that widening a parameter range cannot absorb.

Finally, all results are for a single track, a single vehicle, and a single
arena. The re-centering result in particular produces a specialized policy by
construction, and we have no evidence about how the sensitivity ordering
between observation and dynamics perturbations would change under a
perception-based state estimator, where the observation map is far noisier
and far less systematic than motion capture.

